

# Spatial Query — Complete Requirements & Behavior

## 1. Purpose

The Spatial Query feature must provide a flexible client-side GIS query system where users can select different combinations of **source** and **target layers/features**, apply a spatial relationship, and receive matching source features.

The architecture must **not assume a single source layer or single target layer**. Both sides must ultimately be represented as collections of identifiable features. 

---

# 2. Technology & Constraints

The feature is being added to an existing:

* React.js application
* JavaScript application — **no TypeScript**
* Ant Design UI
* Leaflet.js map
* Turf.js spatial operations
* Flatbush spatial indexes
* Existing chunking hook

Requirements:

1. All spatial processing must happen **client-side**.
2. Do **not** introduce Web Workers.
3. Reuse the existing chunking hook.
4. Reuse existing Flatbush indexes.
5. Do not introduce another spatial-index library.
6. Do not replace Leaflet or Ant Design.
7. Reuse existing map, layer, selection, highlighting, popup, indexing and state-management systems.
8. Do not modify unrelated functionality.
9. Follow the project's existing coding conventions.
10. Do not duplicate existing utilities.
11. Use the already installed library versions.
12. Keep the feature modular and extensible. 

---

# 3. Core Spatial Query Concept

The query consists of:

```text
SOURCE
   │
   │ Spatial Operation
   ▼
TARGET
```

The source can represent:

* Entire layer
* Single feature
* Multiple features from a single layer

The target can represent:

* Single layer
* Single feature
* Multiple features from one layer



The implementation must therefore support:

```text
Layer → Layer (All feature of layer → All feature of layer)
Layer → Feature (All feature → selected feature )
Layer → Multiple Features (All feature → multiple selected feature)

Feature → Layer (selected feature → All feature)
Feature → Feature (selected feature → selected feature)
Feature → Multiple Features (selected feature → multiple selected feature)

Multiple Features → Layer
Multiple Features → Feature
Multiple Features → Multiple Features
```

---

# 4. Source Selection Requirements

## 4.1 Entire Layer

User can select:

```text
Source Type: Layer
Source Layer: Buildings
```

Behavior:

> Every feature in the selected layer becomes a source candidate.

---

## 4.2 Single Feature

User can select:

```text
Source Type: Feature
Layer: Buildings
Feature: Building #1024
```

Behavior:

> Only Building #1024 participates as the source.

---

## 4.3 Multiple Features From One Layer

User can select:

```text
Source Type: Features
Layer: Buildings

✓ Building #101
✓ Building #102
✓ Building #105
✓ Building #110
```

Behavior:

> Only the selected features become source candidates.



---

# 5. Target Selection Requirements

The target must be more flexible than the source.

## 5.1 Single Layer

```text
Target Type: Layer
Target Layer: Flood Zones
```

All features in the layer become target candidates.

---


## 5.3 Multiple Features From One Layer

```text
Target Layer: Flood Zones

✓ Zone #1
✓ Zone #3
✓ Zone #7
```

Only those features participate.

---



## 5.5 Single Target Feature

Example:

```text
Target Type: Feature
Layer: Flood Zones
Feature: Flood Zone #5
```

Only that feature participates.

---


# 7. Multi-Target Behavior

This is one of the most important behavioral requirements.

When multiple targets are selected, the default semantic is:

> **A source feature is a match if it satisfies the selected spatial relationship with at least one target feature.**

Example:

```text
Source:
Building #101

Targets:
Zone #1
Zone #2
Zone #3
```

If:

```text
Building #101 INTERSECTS Zone #2
```

then:

```text
Building #101 = MATCH
```

It does not need to match every target.



---

# 8. Duplicate Result Behavior

If one source matches multiple targets:

```text
Building #101
   ├── Zone #2
   ├── Zone #3
   └── Zone #5
```

the source must appear **only once** in the primary result set.

Do not produce:

```text
Building #101
Building #101
Building #101
```

Instead:

```javascript
{
    source: {...},
    matchedTargets: [...]
}
```



---

# 9. Preserve Match Relationships

Although duplicate source results are removed, the system must retain information about **which targets matched**.

Example:

```javascript
{
    source: {
        layerId: "buildings",
        featureId: "building-101",
        feature: sourceFeature
    },

    matchedTargets: [
        {
            layerId: "flood-zones",
            featureId: "zone-2",
            feature: targetFeature
        },
        {
            layerId: "flood-zones",
            featureId: "zone-3",
            feature: targetFeature
        }
    ],

    operation: "intersects"
}
```

This relationship information must not be discarded. 

---

# 10. Source/Target Normalization

The UI can have different selection modes, but the spatial engine should receive one consistent representation.

For example:

```javascript
{
    source: {
        type: "features",
        items: [
            {
                layerId: "buildings",
                featureId: "building-101",
                feature: {}
            },
            {
                layerId: "buildings",
                featureId: "building-102",
                feature: {}
            }
        ]
    },

    target: {
        type: "features",
        items: [
            {
                layerId: "flood-zones",
                featureId: "zone-1",
                feature: {}
            },
            {
                layerId: "rivers",
                featureId: "river-4",
                feature: {}
            }
        ]
    },

    operation: "intersects"
}
```

The core spatial engine should work against this normalized structure rather than directly against UI state. 

---

# 11. Spatial Operations

The system must support:

* Within
* Contains
* Intersects
* Overlaps
* Touches
* Crosses
* Disjoint
* Within Distance
* Nearest Feature

These operations must not blindly be available for every geometry combination.

The system should determine:

1. Source geometry type
2. Target geometry type
3. Operation compatibility
4. Enable/disable appropriate operations

For incompatible combinations, show a clear message such as:

```text
This spatial operation is not supported
for the selected geometry types.
```



---

# 12. Spatial Operation Behavior

## Within

```text
Source → Target
```

Return source features that are within the target geometry.

Example:

```text
Buildings
    ↓
Within
    ↓
Flood Zones
```

---

## Contains

Return source features that contain the target geometry.

---

## Intersects

Return source features whose geometry intersects at least one target.

---

## Overlaps

Return source features that spatially overlap the target according to the supported geometry semantics.

---

## Touches

Return source features that touch the target geometry.

---

## Crosses

Return source features that cross the target geometry.

---

## Disjoint

Return source features that are disjoint from the relevant target geometry according to the query semantics.

---

## Within Distance

Example:

```text
Source:
Hospital #10

Operation:
Within Distance

Target:
Selected geometry

Distance:
5 km
```

Return source features within the configured distance.

---

## Nearest Feature

Find the nearest relevant target/source feature according to the selected query direction and preserve the relationship in the result.

The acceptance criteria explicitly require both **Within Distance** and **Nearest Feature**. 

---

# 13. Attribute Filtering

The query must support optional attribute filters.

Example source filter:

```text
Type = "Government"
AND
Status = "Active"
```

Target filter:

```text
RiskLevel = "High"
```

Where logically safe, attribute filtering should happen **before expensive spatial calculations**.



---

# 14. Query Configuration

The configuration needs to represent collections rather than single entities.

Conceptually:

```javascript
{
    source: {
        mode: "layer" | "feature" | "features",
        layers: [],
        features: []
    },

    operation: "within",

    target: {
        mode:
            "layer" |
            "layers" |
            "feature" |
            "features" |
            "drawnGeometry",

        layers: [],
        features: [],
        geometries: []
    },

    filters: [],

    distance: null,

    distanceUnit: "kilometers"
}
```

The exact domain model should be adapted to the existing application's architecture rather than copied blindly. 

---

# 15. Critical Architecture Rule

Do **not** design the engine around:

```javascript
const sourceLayer = ...
const targetLayer = ...
const targetFeature = ...
```

Instead, work with:

```javascript
sourceItems[]
targetItems[]
```

Each item should retain:

```javascript
{
    layerId,
    featureId,
    feature
}
```

This is required to support multi-layer and multi-feature queries correctly. 

---

# 16. Flatbush Behavior

Existing Flatbush indexes must be reused.

If the application has:

```text
Layer A → Flatbush A
Layer B → Flatbush B
Layer C → Flatbush C
```

and the target is:

```text
Flood Zones + Rivers + Roads
```

the query engine should search the relevant indexes independently:

```text
                Query Geometry
                      │
       ┌──────────────┼──────────────┐
       ▼              ▼              ▼
 Flood Index      River Index      Road Index
       │              │              │
       ▼              ▼              ▼
 Candidates       Candidates       Candidates
       └──────────────┼──────────────┘
                      ▼
                Exact Turf Tests
                      ▼
                   Results
```

Do not scan every target feature if Flatbush can eliminate candidates first. 

---

# 17. Query Execution Pipeline

The expected execution flow is:

```text
User Query Configuration
          ↓
      Validation
          ↓
 Normalize Source
          ↓
 Normalize Target
          ↓
 Attribute Filtering
          ↓
 Spatial Candidate Search
          ↓
       Flatbush
          ↓
 Candidate Features
          ↓
 Existing Chunking Hook
          ↓
    Exact Turf Tests
          ↓
     Match / No Match
          ↓
 Deduplicate Sources
          ↓
 Preserve Target Matches
          ↓
       Results
          ↓
 ┌────────┼─────────┐
 ↓        ↓         ↓
Map     Table     Export
```



---

# 18. Performance Requirements

For large datasets:

### Must do

* Reuse Flatbush.
* Reuse existing chunking.
* Use bounding-box candidate filtering.
* Apply attribute filters early where safe.
* Run exact Turf operations only on candidates.
* Deduplicate results.
* Avoid unnecessary geometry cloning.
* Avoid unnecessary React state updates.
* Preserve only necessary metadata.

### Must not do

* Source × Target brute-force comparisons when avoidable.
* Rebuild Flatbush unnecessarily.
* Clone entire GeoJSON collections.
* Store huge feature collections in React state unnecessarily.
* Trigger React rendering for every processed feature.
* Add another spatial index.
* Add Web Workers. 

---

# 19. Chunking Behavior

The existing chunking hook must be used for large queries.

Conceptually:

```text
Source Features
      ↓
Candidate Filtering
      ↓
Existing Chunking Hook
      ↓
 ┌────┼────┬────┐
 ↓    ↓    ↓    ↓
C1   C2   C3   ...
 ↓    ↓    ↓
Exact Turf Tests
      ↓
Deduplicated Results
```

Do not create another chunking implementation. 

